.\" procsnoop(8) - trace process fork/exec/exit, filtered by process subtree
.TH PROCSNOOP 8 "October 2026" "procsnoop" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
procsnoop \- trace process fork/exec/exit, filtered by process subtree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B procsnoop
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B procsnoop
traces process creation, execution and termination system\-wide, and prints the
events filtered by process lineage.
It attaches to the kernel's
.BR sched_process_fork ,
.BR sched_process_exec
and
.BR sched_process_exit
tracepoints using eBPF (through the BCC framework), reconstructs the process
tree in user space, and decides for each event whether the subject process
belongs to a selected subtree.
.PP
A
.I root
is a process selected with
.B \-P
or by a pattern
.RB ( \-m , \-f , \-X ).
By default,
.B procsnoop
hides each root together with everything descended from it; with
.B \-o
it prints only those subtrees instead.
.PP
Because the tree is reconstructed from tracepoints rather than sampled from
.IR /proc ,
a process stays attributed to its ancestors even if intermediate processes
never
.BR exec (2)
(for example subshells), exit early, or are reparented.
.PP
.B procsnoop
must run as root.
When started by an unprivileged user it re\-executes itself through
.BR sudo (8),
unless
.B \-\-no\-sudo
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Root selection
.PP
.B \-P
and the pattern options can be combined; a process is a root if it matches any
of them.
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-pid PID[,PID...]
Roots: PID(s) whose subtree is hidden (or, with
.BR \-o ,
shown).
The argument is a comma\-separated list; the option is repeatable.
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-match PATTERN
Roots: processes whose name (the kernel
.IR comm ,
at most 15 characters) matches, like
.BR pgrep .
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-match\-full PATTERN
Roots: processes whose command line (arguments joined by spaces) matches, like
.B pgrep \-f .
.TP
.B \-X, \-\-match\-exe PATTERN
Roots: processes whose executable matches.
The value tested is the loaded binary
.RI ( /proc/ PID /exe,
or the interpreter for scripts), not the script path.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-fixed\-strings
Treat patterns as literal strings rather than regular expressions.
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-exact
Require patterns to match the whole value, like
.B pgrep \-x .
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-ignore\-case
Match patterns case\-insensitively.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-only
Invert the selection: print only the selected subtrees instead of hiding them.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-roots
Count the root processes themselves as part of their subtrees.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-no\-roots
Filter only the descendants; the roots are treated like any other process.
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-events EV[,EV...]
Events to print:
.BR FORK ,
.BR EXEC ,
.B EXIT
or
.BR ALL .
The default is
.BR EXEC,EXIT .
All three event types are always traced internally, regardless of this option.
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-top\-level
Print only processes none of whose ancestors were printed (scripts and
programs, not their subprocesses).
Interactive shells do not count as printed ancestors.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
With
.BR \-T ,
count interactive shells as printed ancestors too, so any session opened after
startup hides everything run in it.
.TP
.B \-\-paired
Print an
.B EXIT
event only for processes whose
.B FORK
or
.B EXEC
line was printed.
This is the default whenever
.B \-\-events
allows it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-paired
Print every
.B EXIT
event that passes the filters.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-hidden
Also print filtered lines, dimmed and marked with an asterisk.
Intended for debugging.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-time {wall,rel,none}
Select the timestamp column:
.B wall
is
.IR HH:MM:SS.mmm ,
.B rel
is the number of seconds since the trace started, and
.B none
omits the column.
The default is
.BR wall .
.TP
.B \-U, \-\-uid
Add a UID column.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-pcomm
Add a column with the parent's name, taken from the tracked tree so it works
even for parents that have already exited.
.TP
.B \-\-exe
Show the loaded binary when it differs from
.IR argv[0] .
.TP
.B \-\-color [{auto,always,never}]
Colorize the output.
The default is
.BR auto ,
which enables color only when writing to a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Do not print the column header.
.SS Tuning
.TP
.B \-\-arg\-bytes N
Maximum number of bytes of
.I argv
captured per
.BR exec .
Must be a power of two between 256 and 16384; the default is 4096.
Larger values capture more of long command lines.
.TP
.B \-\-buffer\-pages N
Size of the BPF ring buffer in pages.
Must be a power of two between 16 and 65536; the default is 1024 (4\ MiB).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sudo
Do not re\-run through
.BR sudo (8)
when not root; fail instead.
.SS Miscellaneous
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal termination.
This includes termination by
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ,
which stop the trace cleanly.
.TP
.B 2
Command\-line usage error.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error (for example the BCC Python module is missing, the process is not
running as root with
.BR \-\-no\-sudo ,
or a requested PID does not exist).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B procsnoop \-T
Print only top\-level commands, not their subprocesses.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-T \-\-strict
Like
.BR \-T ,
but sessions opened after startup are covered too.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-P 1234,5678
Hide processes 1234 and 5678 and everything below them.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-P "$(pgrep \-d, \-x sshd)" \-e all
Hide sshd and its subtree, printing all three event types.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-o \-P 4242
Show only process 4242 and its subtree.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-P 2 \-e all
Hide kernel threads (kthreadd's subtree).
.TP
.B procsnoop \-m '^(chrome|code)$'
Hide Chrome and VS Code and everything they start.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-o \-f 'make .*\-j' \-T
Show only the
.B make \-j...
invocations themselves.
.TP
.B procsnoop \-F \-\-exact \-X /usr/bin/python3.12
Match the interpreter path literally and exactly.
.SH NOTES
.B procsnoop
requires Linux 5.8 or newer (for the BPF ring buffer) and the BCC Python module
.RI ( python3\-bpfcc
on Debian/Ubuntu,
.B python3\-bcc
on Fedora,
.B python\-bcc
on Arch).
.PP
If the ring buffer overflows, events are lost and a warning is printed;
the reported lineage may then be incomplete.
Increase
.B \-\-buffer\-pages
to reduce the chance of this.
.PP
Process names and command\-line arguments are attacker\-controlled; control
characters are escaped before they reach the terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pgrep (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR bpftrace (8),
.BR execsnoop (8)

